namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup reduction

\section reduction-single Single-array reductions

Simple reduction operations consist in computing a single value from an collection of elements,
like their minimum value or sum.
Linx provides several such operations, and supports custom reductions using the `reduce()` function.
A reducer is passed as argument, which must be a diadic function for which `identity_element(reducer)` is defined.
The library comes with many compatible functors, like `Add`, `Multiply` or `Max`.
See the `identity_element()` overloads at the bottom of this page for a complete list.
Many builtin reduction functions like `sum()`, `product()` or `max()` are also listed there.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp single

It is also possible to apply a transform to each element prior to reduction.
Transform and reduction are performed in one pass.
The dedicated function `transform_reduce()` takes as input a monadic transform in addition to the reducer,
which can be any type of Kokkos-compatible function: free function, lambda or functor.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp transform

\section reduction-multiple Multi-array reductions

Actually, `transform_reduce()` accepts an arbitrary number of input arrays.
When several of them are passed in, the transform must accept as input as many arguments:
it will be fed with one element per array.
Again, builtin functions like `dot()` or `distance()` are available and listed below.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp multiple

*/

}
